<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Titus Quinctius Flamininus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 229–174 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Flamininus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Titus Quinctius Flamininus was a Roman general and politician who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmian-games">Isthmian Games</a> of 196 BCE he proclaimed the freedom of the Greek cities from Macedonian rule, and Roman forces left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 194 BCE. He was elected consul in 198 BCE and censor in 189 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus.jpg" alt="Titus Quinctius Flamininus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael J. taylor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flamininus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
